Every essay in this series has fixed one wall: the boundary around the factory, the approvals, the measures, the incentives, the core. A board doesn't meet those walls one essay at a time. It meets them one month at a time, in a board pack, over the first year.

{% yearlanes /%}

For six months the board pack has little to show, and in month four it has nothing at all. Underneath, almost everything that will matter later is being built.

*This is the last essay in a series on digital transformation in banking. It puts the first five on a single calendar: [the factory](/blog/the-factory-is-the-easy-part), [evidence](/blog/evidence-not-meetings), [the one number](/blog/the-one-number-a-board-should-watch), [fear](/blog/fear-is-rational) and [the core](/blog/fast-edge-steady-core).*

## The shape nobody puts in the business case

{% jcurve /%}

Two very different kinds of research found the same shape. The economists Erik Brynjolfsson, Daniel Rock and Chad Syverson call it the productivity J-curve: new technology needs investment in skills, processes and ways of working that measurements miss, so results look worse before they look better. DORA found it inside technology teams. Halfway through a transformation, teams were doing more manual work, not less, as automation surfaced technical debt and new testing needs.

Both times I've been through a first year, the hardest conversation with the board came in the dip: the factory busy, the board pack thin, and lead time moving the wrong way.

## Change 27's year

{% changeyear /%}

It got worse first. In month four, change 27 took ten days longer than when the year began, because three new squads were feeding the same boundary, exactly as the first essay's arithmetic predicts. Then the fixes landed, roughly one a month, and every one of them shortened a wait rather than the work.

{% pullquote quote="The factory's part of change 27 took an afternoon in month one, and an afternoon in month twelve. Everything that improved was around it." /%}

## One question a quarter

A board can't steer a J-curve by watching launches. It can by asking the right question at the right time.

{% quarterquestions /%}

## What boards get wrong in year one

{% cardgrid label="The expectation" %}
- **Features by month three.** The first quarter builds the road, not the traffic. A factory that ships features before its pipeline ships them slowly for years.
- **More squads, more speed.** Until the boundary is funded, more squads mean a longer queue. In this year, lead time peaked the month after three squads joined.
- **Push harder in the dip.** The dip is the investment. Cutting the platform or the boundary at month four locks in the bottom of the J.
- **The factory will fix the rest.** Every day taken off change 27 was waiting time, not working time.
{% /cardgrid %}

## Running year one

{% steps %}
1. **Put the J in the business case.** Show the dip, and the month you expect the turn, before the first squad starts.
2. **Fund the boundary in the same budget as the factory.** Evidence pipelines, approvals, operations and the edge layer.
3. **Report one number from month one.** Lead time from idea to customer, honestly, even while it rises.
4. **Land one fix a month around the factory.** Each one should show up in the number within weeks.
5. **Rewrite one rule a quarter.** And review the first fast failure in the open, without blame.
{% /steps %}

## What I'd tell a board

{% boardcard title="Five things to know about year one" %}
1. **Expect a J, not a line.** It gets worse before it gets better. Agree in advance what the turn will look like.
2. **The dip is the investment.** Platforms, pipelines and evidence never appear on a launch calendar; they appear in everything after.
3. **Don't cut the boundary at month four.** That's how transformations lock in the bottom of the J.
4. **Watch one number from day one.** Lead time from idea to customer, reported honestly, even when it rises.
5. **Ask one question a quarter.** Platform, evidence, flow, rules: the right question keeps the board on the work that matters.
{% /boardcard %}

## Year one, scored

{% yearscoreboard /%}

Twelve months, five walls, one sentence on a screen. The last line is the one I'd frame. The core still releases once a quarter, and nothing waits for it. The transformation didn't make the bank reckless. It made the bank's caution precise.

And the series ends where it began. The factory was the easy part. Everything that made change 27 faster happened around it.

---

### Sources

- Erik Brynjolfsson, Daniel Rock and Chad Syverson, "[The Productivity J-Curve: How Intangibles Complement General Purpose Technologies](https://doi.org/10.1257/mac.20180386)," *American Economic Journal: Macroeconomics* 13(1), 2021, 333–372.
- DORA, *[Accelerate State of DevOps Report 2018](https://dora.dev/research/2018/dora-report/)*, pp. 29–30: the "J-curve of transformation" and manual work by performance level.
- The earlier essays in this series, whose models supply every number in "Change 27's year" and the scoreboard.
